WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.019 --> 00:00:14.699
This fine-looking vessel is the MS München.

00:00:14.699 --> 00:00:20.320
At over a quarter of a kilometre long, she’s
roughly the same size as the Titanic and boasts

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:26.980
a capacity of more than 45,000 metric tons
- that’s big enough to transport four and

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:30.480
a half million good-sized badgers…

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:36.810
On the 7th of December 1978, the München
departed from Bremerhaven, Germany, headed

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:38.510
for Savannah, Georgia.

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:43.480
A fierce storm had been raging across the
North Atlantic for some weeks at the time,

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.030
but the captain of the München wasn’t worried.

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:50.510
His huge ship had been designed to handle
stormy weather with ease.

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:56.260
In fact, thanks to certain properties of her
design, the MS München was considered practically

00:00:56.260 --> 00:01:00.200
unsinkable… some people never learn.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:06.200
So, unperturbed by the weather, the 28-man
crew of the München charted a course for

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:25.950
the heart of the storm… but neither ship
nor crew ever came out 

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:32.380
the other side.

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:56.669
As 

00:01:56.669 --> 00:02:42.220
the winds 

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:47.390
in the North Atlantic began to die down, a
huge search effort was launched involving

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:50.860
13 aircraft and some 80 ships.

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:57.250
Traces of the München were found - including
several eerily empty lifeboats - but it soon

00:02:57.250 --> 00:03:01.310
became clear the ship had been taken by the
storm.

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:07.620
To this day, the giant vessel remains lost
somewhere on the bottom of the Atlantic Ocean.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:11.820
An investigation was launched to uncover what
could possibly have happened to sink such

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:13.620
an enormous vessel.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:18.100
And what it turned up was… nothing.

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:20.520
Or at least, nothing that made any sense.

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:26.770
There were no convenient icebergs nearby to
blame things on, and nobody could come up

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:33.100
with a plausible explanation as to how a vessel
considered practically unsinkable could have

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:36.330
been eaten by a North Atlantic storm.

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:42.510
In the end, the investigation concluded that
a ‘freak weather event’ was to blame - which

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:50.680
is maritime shorthand for ‘we-literally-have-no-idea-what-happened-but-this-is-a-mandatory-field-and-a-I-have-to-write-something’.

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:56.530
The investigation did uncover one weird little
detail though - one of the drifting lifeboats

00:03:56.530 --> 00:04:03.400
showed severe damage to the launching mechanism
used to release it into the water in an emergency.

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:08.850
The damage seemed to imply that the lifeboat
hadn’t been actively launched by the München’s

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:15.650
crew, but had instead been torn from the ship’s
side with enough force to bend the set of

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:19.470
solid steel pins holding it in place.

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:24.340
And that made absolutely no sense whatsoever.

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:29.759
Because the only thing in the middle of the
North Atlantic powerful enough to bend solid

00:04:29.759 --> 00:04:36.750
steel was the crushing force of a huge wave.

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:40.990
But, while still attached to the ship’s
launching mechanism, the lifeboat would have

00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:47.050
hung some 20 metres above the water - far
higher than any wave could ever have reached.

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:51.370
Or at least, that’s what oceanographers
at the time believed.

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:57.810
You see, up until really quite recently, we
humans were under the impression that ocean

00:04:57.810 --> 00:05:03.580
wave heights strictly followed a classic normal
distribution - a bell curve.

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:09.930
If that were true, the vast majority of waves
in any given stretch of ocean should be roughly

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:12.490
the same height as those around them.

00:05:12.490 --> 00:05:18.279
Larger or smaller exceptions would be possible,
but increasingly unlikely the further they

00:05:18.279 --> 00:05:20.919
got from the average wave height.

00:05:20.919 --> 00:05:27.219
As you may know, wave height largely depends
on 3 factors: wind speed, wind duration, and

00:05:27.219 --> 00:05:33.569
the distance over which that wind blows in
one direction, also known as the ‘fetch’.

00:05:33.569 --> 00:05:38.660
Considering these variables, the MS München
should have been facing waves of around 10

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:41.999
to 12 metres the night she was lost.

00:05:41.999 --> 00:05:49.350
The idea that among those regular, 10 metre
waves, a 20 metre monster could suddenly have

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:55.999
appeared to tear one of the lifeboats clean
off its launching gear was considered so statistically

00:05:55.999 --> 00:06:00.409
unlikely as to have been essentially impossible.

00:06:00.409 --> 00:06:04.310
But not everyone was quite so sceptical.

00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:10.789
Because despite what the maths said, there
were rumours that plenty of experienced seamen

00:06:10.789 --> 00:06:15.689
claimed to have come across random giant waves
out in the open ocean.

00:06:15.689 --> 00:06:20.840
According to the stories, these so-called
‘rogue’ waves seemed to appear out of

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:26.310
nowhere and, far bigger than their peers,
struck with enough force to sink some of the

00:06:26.310 --> 00:06:34.340
biggest, best-equipped vessels ever built…
vessels like the MS München, for example.

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:39.060
But these tall tales were taken with a generous
dose of sea salt by the scientific community

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:46.009
- after all, the maths was clear: a random
giant wave among a stretch of far smaller

00:06:46.009 --> 00:06:51.029
waves just wasn’t a realistic statistical
possibility.

00:06:51.029 --> 00:06:56.990
For that reason, the strange sinking of the
MS München was largely forgotten for some

00:06:56.990 --> 00:07:05.659
17 years until, on New Year’s Day in 1995,
something remarkable happened that changed

00:07:05.659 --> 00:07:09.860
the way science thought about oceanic waves
forever.

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:16.300
This minor maritime miracle took place at
the Draupner offshore oil rig some 100 miles

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:18.020
off the coast of Norway.

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:23.199
A massive hurricane was sweeping across the
North Sea at the time, bringing with it enormous

00:07:23.199 --> 00:07:24.199
waves.

00:07:24.199 --> 00:07:28.389
The rig had been fitted with state-of-the-art
equipment capable of accurately measuring

00:07:28.389 --> 00:07:34.729
the height of those waves, and, as oceanographers
of the day would have expected, each passing

00:07:34.729 --> 00:07:40.370
peak was approximately the same height as
the last - around 12 metres, which is about

00:07:40.370 --> 00:07:42.729
the size of a 4-story building.

00:07:42.729 --> 00:07:50.560
But then, out of nowhere, the recording gear
picked up a 26 metre monster wave, more than

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:55.009
twice as tall and steep as everything around
it.

00:07:55.009 --> 00:08:00.400
This mutant wave smashed against the oil rig,
damaging structures that were never expected

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:02.999
to be anywhere near the waves below.

00:08:02.999 --> 00:08:07.930
When scientists came to analyse readings from
their equipment sometime later, they were

00:08:07.930 --> 00:08:14.439
forced to come to a vexing conclusion - the
Draupner oil rig had been struck by a wave

00:08:14.439 --> 00:08:18.089
so big it shouldn’t have existed.

00:08:18.089 --> 00:08:24.289
The Draupner wave, as it came to be known,
was the first rogue wave ever recorded by

00:08:24.289 --> 00:08:26.689
reliable scientific instruments.

00:08:26.689 --> 00:08:32.250
It was the oceanic equivalent of a camera
trap in the Himalayas snapping a picture of

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:33.250
a yeti.

00:08:33.250 --> 00:08:40.310
A creature once considered a myth had suddenly
been shown to be very real indeed, and scientists

00:08:40.310 --> 00:08:46.800
who had confidently rejected stories of rogue
waves for decades suddenly found themselves

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:49.570
frantically revisiting their calculations.

00:08:49.570 --> 00:08:54.040
There was, however, still hope of scientific
salvation.

00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:58.840
After all, extreme outcomes aren’t impossible
in a normal distribution of wave heights,

00:08:58.840 --> 00:09:01.760
they’re just statistically unlikely.

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:07.400
Some brave soul went away and crunched the
numbers, and it turned out something as big

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:13.570
as the Draupner wave should only come along
once every 20,000 years or so.

00:09:13.570 --> 00:09:20.320
Which raised an important question: was the
Draupner wave a statistical anomaly - an incredibly

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:28.760
unlikely freak event… or a sign that our
assumptions about giant rogue waves were utterly

00:09:28.760 --> 00:09:30.710
incorrect?

00:09:30.710 --> 00:09:36.940
That question wasn’t only interesting to
oceanographers and theoreticians - its implications

00:09:36.940 --> 00:09:38.530
were truly frightening.

00:09:38.530 --> 00:09:43.500
You see, the Draupner oil platform had been
designed to withstand what scientists at the

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:50.820
time believed was a once-in-10,000-year expected
maximum wave height of 20 metres.

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:55.490
The rig survived the blow with only minor
damage, but it wasn’t the only thing built

00:09:55.490 --> 00:10:02.950
to design standards based on the idea that
very large waves were exceptionally rare - pretty

00:10:02.950 --> 00:10:07.260
much every ship on earth was, too.

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:12.470
In fact, most were designed to withstand waves
of just 15 metres.

00:10:12.470 --> 00:10:17.750
If waves like the one that hit the Draupner
oil rig were more common than anyone had previously

00:10:17.750 --> 00:10:23.930
thought, the majority of mankind’s collective
fleet was at risk.

00:10:23.930 --> 00:10:29.800
In December 2000, the EU launched a project
called MaxWave to try and find out just how

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:33.680
common these so-called rogue waves might be.

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:38.400
As part of the project, they studied global
satellite data covering a 3-week period in

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:43.060
order to carry out what was basically a rogue
wave census.

00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:50.020
Despite the relatively poor resolution of
the satellite images, the team found not one,

00:10:50.020 --> 00:10:57.190
not two, but ten rogue waves of 25 metres
or more.

00:10:57.190 --> 00:11:04.030
It was a pretty terrifying revelation, and
yet today we know that rogue waves – defined

00:11:04.030 --> 00:11:08.810
as waves that are twice as high as the so-called
‘significant wave height’ of the waves

00:11:08.810 --> 00:11:14.030
around them - are far more common even than
that.

00:11:14.030 --> 00:11:22.200
In fact, they occur every single day in every
ocean on planet earth.

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:28.790
Some scientists believe as many as 1 in 3000
waves ‘go rogue.’

00:11:28.790 --> 00:11:31.830
Just when you thought it was safe to go back
into the water.

00:11:31.830 --> 00:11:36.910
One little quirk of the rogue wave mythology
is that they appear to be a relatively modern

00:11:36.910 --> 00:11:43.680
phenomenon – the earliest mentions of such
waves come out of the early 19th century.

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:49.480
Considering we humans have been pissing around
in boats for a good 10,000 years, that’s

00:11:49.480 --> 00:11:50.500
a little odd.

00:11:50.500 --> 00:11:53.690
But then again, it probably shouldn’t be
all that surprising.

00:11:53.690 --> 00:11:59.800
When the Queen Elizabeth II, a cruise liner
affectionately known as the QE2 to most Brits,

00:11:59.800 --> 00:12:05.650
came face to face with a rogue wave during
a hurricane in the North Atlantic in 1995,

00:12:05.650 --> 00:12:11.650
her captain described the wave as resembling
the white cliffs of Dover - a literal wall

00:12:11.650 --> 00:12:16.360
of water that bore down on his ship with frightening
force.

00:12:16.360 --> 00:12:21.830
The QE2 survived the encounter, but pretty
much any vessel built before the advent of

00:12:21.830 --> 00:12:27.930
steel double-hulled ships in the 20th century
almost certainly would not have.

00:12:27.930 --> 00:12:34.060
To put that another way, those who came across
rogue waves throughout most of human history

00:12:34.060 --> 00:12:36.530
probably didn’t live to tell the tale.

00:12:36.530 --> 00:12:39.500
Now, I know all of this sounds pretty scary.

00:12:39.500 --> 00:12:44.090
Thousands of ship-killing waves on the loose
in the world’s oceans and all that.

00:12:44.090 --> 00:12:49.490
But before you go and cancel that lovely cruise
you had planned, remember: a rogue wave is

00:12:49.490 --> 00:12:55.320
at least twice the height of its peers - that
means it’s only monstrous compared to the

00:12:55.320 --> 00:12:57.570
current sea state.

00:12:57.570 --> 00:13:03.010
If you’re on a ship in stormy seas where
the average wave is a 15-metre behemoth, a

00:13:03.010 --> 00:13:05.930
rogue wave could well spell your end.

00:13:05.930 --> 00:13:12.040
If, however, you’re cruising through crystal
clear and calm waters in the Caribbean, a

00:13:12.040 --> 00:13:16.840
rogue wave probably won’t even make you
spill your Pina colada.

00:13:16.840 --> 00:13:21.800
On top of that, it’s important to realise
that rogue waves are also exclusively fleeting

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:22.800
phenomena.

00:13:22.800 --> 00:13:27.950
Unlike tsunamis, they don’t travel across
huge stretches of open ocean.

00:13:27.950 --> 00:13:32.250
They form rapidly and dissipate almost as
fast.

00:13:32.250 --> 00:13:37.370
Common they may be, but you’d still have
to get incredibly unlucky to actually come

00:13:37.370 --> 00:13:38.370
across one.

00:13:38.370 --> 00:13:40.980
So, why do they form so fast?

00:13:40.980 --> 00:13:43.460
Come to think of it, why do they form at all?

00:13:43.460 --> 00:13:50.200
If the mathematics of wave formation suggests
such giant waves shouldn’t really exist,

00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:51.200
why do they?

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:56.081
As I mentioned earlier, oceanic waves are
largely formed by the wind, and you’d expect

00:13:56.081 --> 00:14:01.980
all the waves in a given stretch of sea to
be subjected to roughly the same wind conditions.

00:14:01.980 --> 00:14:05.960
If you watched my recent video on the lighthouse
keepers who went missing on the Flannan Isles

00:14:05.960 --> 00:14:11.320
one hundred and twenty years ago, you’ll
already know what I’m going to say here

00:14:11.320 --> 00:14:17.380
- the truth is, we still aren’t 100% sure
what causes rogue waves.

00:14:17.380 --> 00:14:21.920
Some researchers believe waves travelling
at different speeds can essentially ‘join

00:14:21.920 --> 00:14:28.870
forces’ to create a monster, while others
think intersecting currents might be the answer.

00:14:28.870 --> 00:14:33.350
But ultimately there are dozens of different
theories out there to choose from.

00:14:33.350 --> 00:14:39.290
It may well be that there’s no single process
that causes rogue waves, and that many different

00:14:39.290 --> 00:14:44.720
sea conditions can coax these ship-killing
giants into existence.

00:14:44.720 --> 00:14:50.880
As for whether a rogue wave sank the München
- it’s widely accepted that this apparently

00:14:50.880 --> 00:14:59.010
unsinkable ship - designed to shrug off rough
seas with ease - most likely was indeed sunk

00:14:59.010 --> 00:15:01.950
by a monumental rogue wave.

00:15:01.950 --> 00:15:09.070
A colossal wall of water perhaps 30 metres
high that reared up out of the night and devoured

00:15:09.070 --> 00:15:10.090
the ship.

00:15:10.090 --> 00:15:14.480
Unsurprisingly, the München isn’t the only
vessel to have been sunk by a rogue wave.

00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:20.820
In the last couple of decades of the 20th
century, more than 200 supertankers greater

00:15:20.820 --> 00:15:25.950
than 200 metres in length were lost to bad
weather around the world.

00:15:25.950 --> 00:15:32.530
It’s now thought that rogue waves were likely
the major cause behind many of those disasters,

00:15:32.530 --> 00:15:37.580
accounting for hundreds, perhaps thousands
of lives lost at sea.

00:15:37.580 --> 00:15:42.029
Today, scientists are studying rogue waves
in the hope that by better understanding them,

00:15:42.029 --> 00:15:47.070
we might be able to predict when and where
they’re most likely to form - you know,

00:15:47.070 --> 00:15:49.690
to ensure we’re nowhere bloody near when
they do.

00:15:49.690 --> 00:15:55.560
But the processes involved are extremely complex,
and predicting rogue wave formation in real

00:15:55.560 --> 00:15:59.400
time is going to be a significant challenge.

00:15:59.400 --> 00:16:05.290
Before I go, I have one more piece of exciting
news for all you sailing enthusiasts out there

00:16:05.290 --> 00:16:11.650
- it turns out scientists now think ‘super’
rogue waves up to 5 times the size of the

00:16:11.650 --> 00:16:14.340
waves around them might be a thing.

00:16:14.340 --> 00:16:19.310
Considering your bog-standard rogue wave is
apparently capable of sinking a supertanker

00:16:19.310 --> 00:16:25.760
with ease, we can only guess at the kind of
devastation a super rogue wave would be capable

00:16:25.760 --> 00:16:26.760
of.

00:16:26.760 --> 00:16:33.120
But hey, if you ever find yourself on a ship
far out at sea watching the horizon darken

00:16:33.120 --> 00:16:40.820
like that scene in Interstellar, at least
now you’ll know what’s about to kill you.

00:16:40.820 --> 00:17:08.290
Thanks for watching.

